  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAn upper level low is located over southern Ontario and western\nQuebec early this morning. It will be slowly sliding eastward\nthrough the day today, allowing the trough axis to pass across\nthe region. With the cyclonic flow and cold pool aloft, showers\nhave been ongoing early this morning across the western and\ncentral Adirondacks, where some lake enhancement has been\noccurring off Lake Ontario as well. Through the day today, some\nadditional showers are expected, mainly for northern and western\nparts of the region. The highest coverage looks to be during the\nlate morning through afternoon hours. Precipitation will\ndiminish for late in the day as the best forcing starts to shift\neastward. \n\n3km HRRR suggest there may be just enough SBCAPE (around 500 \nJ/kg), so can't rule out some rumbles of thunder within the \nheaviest showers this afternoon. However, the limited growth \nand weak shear will prevent any storms from getting organized or\nvery strong. With relatively low dewpoints and limited moisture\nin place, no flooding is expected as well. \n\nOtherwise, it will remain partly to mostly cloudy and\ncomfortable today with valley highs only in the 70s. Dewpoints\nwill be in the 50s to lower 60s, so it won't feel too humid. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAfter a dry day on Wednesday, another upper level disturbance\nwill pass close to the region for Thursday into Friday, along\nwith a surface frontal system. Another round of showers and\nthunderstorms is expected across the region each day, especially\nin the afternoon and evening hours on Thursday and again ahead\nof the passing front for during the day on Friday. PWAT values \nwill be increasing, with valley areas seeing max values in the \n1.50\" to 1.75\" range (according to the latest 00z GFS). This is \nabout 1-2 STD above normal. Models suggest that there could be\nenough instability in place for some storms, although the best\ninstability will probably be located south of the region. Some \nof the AI/ML guidance suggest a low-end threat for some strong \nstorms across our area as well, so will need to watch model \ntrends over the next few days. \n\nDrier weather looks to return behind the storm's cold front for\nthe upcoming weekend. Temperatures look to remain seasonable\nbehind the cold front as well.",
